[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 18, "caption": "Figure 1 -An overview of the PRISM-12M dataset and the TissueCraftAI framework. (a) Representative image pairs from the PRISM-12M dataset. Left, whole slide image (WSI) of spatial proteomics data overlaid with a pixel coordinate grid. The tissue was stained with antiCD45 (red, a pan-hematopoietic marker), anti-vimentin (green, a mesenchymal/stromal cell marker) antibodies, and DAPI (blue, a nuclear stain). Right, the corresponding WSI of Hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E) staining, showing tissue morphology. The same coordinate grid is shown, demonstrating spatial alignment with the spatial omics data. Three representative regions of interest (Patches 1-3) are shown at identical pixel coordinates (Width, Height) from both images. (b) Pie chart showing the composition of the PRISM-12M dataset, consisting of over 12 million patches of matched H&E and spatial omics images covering 14 human and mouse tissues. Mouse tissue is indicated with a 'mm' in the tissue name. The proportion of each dataset is indicated in parentheses. (c) Breakdown of the PRISM-12M dataset by tissue source and spatial omics data modality. CyCIF, cyclic Immunofluorescence; CODEX, CO-Detection by", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled **Fig. 1** with panels **a-e**.\n\nPanel **a** compares a **Spatial Omics Whole Slide Image** labeled **CD45, Vimentin, DAPI** with an **H&E Whole Slide Image**. Both show whole-slide tissue images with pixel axes and colored boxed regions. Below each whole-slide image are three extracted image patches labeled **Patch 1**, **Patch 2**, and **Patch 3**, with coordinate annotations. The lower row is labeled **Spatial Omics Image Patch** on the left and **H&E Image Patch** on the right.\n\nPanel **b** is a pie chart titled **PRISM-12M**, showing tissue or disease source categories including **Colon**, **Skin**, **Thymus**, **Tonsil**, **Heart**, **Kidney**, **Lung**, **Sarcoma**, **Liver**, **Bone marrow**, **Bone marrow**, **Pancreas**, **Glioma**, and **Neuroblastoma**. Colon is the largest segment at **45.4%**, followed by Skin at **10.1%**.\n\nPanel **c** is a horizontal bar chart of **Number of Patches** by source/tissue and technology. The legend includes **CODEX**, **CyCIF**, and **Xenium**. The largest category is **Colon (CyCIF)** with about **5,010,049** patches, followed by **Skin (CyCIF)** with about **1,260,894**. Other listed sources include Thymus, Tonsil, Heart, Colon, Kidney, Sarcoma, Liver, Mouse_Bone, Lung, Bone_marrow, Pancreas, and Glioma.\n\nPanel **d** is a model workflow diagram for **TissueCraftAI**. Inputs include a **Conditioning Image** labeled **H&E / DAPI** and a **Text Prompt** example: “Predict CD18 protein expression”. The pipeline shows a **ControlNet Module** with **Image Encoder** producing **Morphological Features**, a **Text Encoding** block with **Text Encoder** producing **Semantic Features**, and a **Latent Diffusion Module** with **Initial Noise Vector**, **Cross Attention**, **Iterative Denoising**, **Conditioned Representation**, and **VAE Decoder**. Outputs are labeled **Proteomics / Transcriptomics Map / Pathology Image**.\n\nPanel **e** lists applications with example thumbnails: **H&E generation**, **Protein expression prediction**, **RNA expression prediction**, **Cell type clustering/annotation**, and **Outcome prediction**."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 19, "caption": "Figure 2 -Improved histology image synthesis from nuclear staining image. (a) Box plot of Pearson Correlation Coefficients (PCCs) between ground truth images and images generated by HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI. n=462,549 image patches. Mean values are indicated. P-values were calculated using a one-sided t-test. (b) Box plot of Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) scores for the three methods using all test CODEX datasets. Lower values indicate better image quality. n=8 datasets. (c) Representative synthetic H&E images. For three different input DAPI images, the corresponding ground truth H&E images are displayed alongside the synthesized images generated by HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI. The PCCs are shown for each synthesized image. Arrowheads indicate representative image imperfections compared to the ground truth.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel figure labeled **Fig. 2** with panels **a**, **b**, and **c**.\n\nPanel **a** shows a boxplot comparing **Pearson Correlation Coefficient** across three methods: **HistoPlexer**, **Pix2Pix-Turbo**, and **TissueCraftAI**. The displayed median/summary values are approximately **0.825**, **0.843**, and **0.915**, respectively. Statistical comparison brackets show p-values including **p=0.024** and **p=0.0076**. TissueCraftAI has the highest correlation.\n\nPanel **b** shows a bar chart comparing **FID Score** across the same three methods. Values are approximately **262.8** for HistoPlexer, **209.0** for Pix2Pix-Turbo, and **138.4** for TissueCraftAI. Statistical brackets show p-values including **p=0.00039** and **p=0.00015**. TissueCraftAI has the lowest FID score.\n\nPanel **c** shows qualitative image examples in a grid. Columns are labeled **Input (DAPI)**, **Ground Truth**, **HistoPlexer**, **Pix2Pix-Turbo**, and **TissueCraftAI**. The input images are grayscale DAPI fluorescence-like images showing nuclei/cell-density patterns. The ground truth and generated outputs are pink/purple histology-like tissue images, likely H&E-style, with adipose-like white spaces and cellular structures. Each row compares model-generated histology transformations from DAPI input against ground truth. Output panels include visible **PCC** annotations, such as **0.825**, **0.890**, **0.900**, **0.797**, **0.860**, **0.912**, **0.885**, **0.844**, and **0.941**. Black arrowheads mark local differences or structures in generated images.\n\nOverall, the figure compares image-to-image translation methods for generating histology-like tissue images from DAPI input, showing TissueCraftAI achieves higher Pearson correlation, lower FID, and visually closer outputs than HistoPlexer and Pix2Pix-Turbo."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 20, "caption": "Figure 3 -Prediction of spatial protein expression from histology images. Performance evaluation using the human bone marrow CODEX dataset. Bar plot showing the per-marker Pearson Correlation Coefficient (PCC) between the ground truth CODEX images and the synthesized images by three methods: HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix, and TissueCraftAI. Error bars represent the standard deviation. n=16,325 image patches. (b) Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) scores for the three methods. Error bars represent standard deviation. n=54 protein markers. (c)", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure labeled “Fig. 3” with panels a-e comparing computational image-to-marker prediction models.\n\nPanel a: Bar chart of Pearson correlation coefficients across protein markers. X-axis lists markers including CD11B, CD15, CYPC, CD3e, CD45, DAPI, CD3, CD33, CD99, MPO, CD138, CD10, CD68, CD4, CD14, HIF1A, SPINK2, CD56, HLA-DR, and ASMA. Legend compares HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI. TissueCraftAI generally has higher positive correlations than the other methods for many markers.\n\nPanel b: Bar chart of FID Score for HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI. TissueCraftAI has the lowest FID score, Pix2Pix-Turbo intermediate, and HistoPlexer highest. Significance brackets show p=0.028 and p=0.011.\n\nPanel c: Qualitative image comparison. Columns are Input (H&E), Ground Truth, HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI. Rows show marker predictions for CD15 and CD3e with DAPI nuclear staining in blue and marker signal in red. Biological source appears to be histology tissue with hematoxylin/eosin input and multiplex/immunofluorescence-like ground truth. TissueCraftAI outputs visually closer red/blue cellular marker distributions and higher displayed PCC values than HistoPlexer and Pix2Pix-Turbo: for CD15, PCC 0.17, 0.15, and 0.50 respectively; for CD3e, PCC 0.11, 0.14, and 0.53 respectively.\n\nPanel d: Bar chart of Pearson correlation coefficient comparing Pix2Pix-Turbo and TissueCraftAI across markers including GPC5, MRP4, HBEGF, BMP7, MYOD, TTN, TOX, PHOX2B, HCNA, NRP, C1Q, VSL, TTN2, PIEZO, MYOG, Hecobsn, PPP2R2C, CD138, DAPI, and LRP1. Green percentage annotations indicate TissueCraftAI improvements over Pix2Pix-Turbo for many markers.\n\nPanel e: Scatter plot comparing TissueCraftAI mean correlation versus Pix2Pix-Turbo mean correlation. Diagonal reference line indicates equal performance. Points are colored by mean improvement percentage, with many points above the diagonal. Labeled examples include GPC5, BMP7, MRP4, HBEGF, and MYOD. Finding: TissueCraftAI shows higher mean correlation than Pix2Pix-Turbo for many markers."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 21, "caption": "Figure 4 -Spatially resolved cell type annotation of histology images with TissueCraftAI. (a) Boxplots comparing the similarity of cell type clusterings using the human bone marrow CODEX dataset. Similarity to the ground truth images is determined using the Fowlkes-Mallows Index (FMI) score for H&E images and synthetic CODEX images generated by HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI. n=16,325 image patches. ( b) Boxplots showing the F1-scores for cell type classification on the bone marrow dataset, comparing predictions by the four", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure labeled “Fig. 4” with panels **a-f**.\n\nPanels **a-c** show boxplots comparing methods:\n- **a:** FMI Score for H&E, HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI. TissueCraftAI appears highest; p-values are annotated above pairwise comparisons.\n- **b:** Macro F1 for the same methods. TissueCraftAI appears highest, followed by HistoPlexer/Pix2Pix-Turbo, with H&E lowest.\n- **c:** Macro F1 for another task or dataset, again comparing H&E, HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI. TissueCraftAI has a wider distribution and higher upper values; p-values are annotated.\n\nPanels **d-f** show qualitative spatial cell-type prediction examples:\n- Each row includes an **Input (H&E)** histology patch, **Ground Truth**, and outputs from **H&E**, **HistoPlexer**, **Pix2Pix-Turbo**, and **TissueCraftAI**.\n- Outputs are plotted as spatial scatter maps with colored cell-type labels.\n- Panel **d** includes scores in method titles: H&E 0.22, HistoPlexer 0.30, Pix2Pix-Turbo 0.23, TissueCraftAI 0.35.\n- Panel **e** includes scores: H&E 0.06, HistoPlexer 0.13, Pix2Pix-Turbo 0.16, TissueCraftAI 0.29.\n- Panel **f** includes scores: H&E 0.19, HistoPlexer 0.19, Pix2Pix-Turbo 0.19, TissueCraftAI 0.40.\n\nVisible biological source objects:\n- H&E-stained tissue microscopy patches.\n- Spatial distributions of cell annotations/classes.\n- Legends include cell types such as B cell, epithelial cell, myeloid cell, neutrophil, NK cell, plasma B, stromal cell, T cell, endothelial cell, malignant cell, and TAM.\n\nVisible transformation/model comparison:\n- H&E input images are used directly or transformed/predicted by model pipelines including HistoPlexer, Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI.\n- The figure compares inferred spatial cell-type maps against ground truth.\n\nMain visible finding:\n- TissueCraftAI generally shows higher quantitative scores and qualitative spatial maps closer to ground truth than H&E-only, HistoPlexer, and Pix2Pix-Turbo in the shown examples."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 22, "caption": "Figure 5 -Prediction of transcript expression using histology images. (a) Box plot showing", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nFigure 5 contains multi-panel scientific results comparing spot detection performance between Pix2Pix-Turbo and TissueCraftAI.\n\nPanel a: Boxplot of “Spot Detection F1 Score” for Pix2Pix-Turbo versus TissueCraftAI. TissueCraftAI shows higher median F1 score. A statistical annotation reports p=0.0002.\n\nPanel b: Bar chart of “Spot Detection F1 Score” across multiple gene/protein markers or targets, with orange bars for Pix2Pix-Turbo and blue bars for TissueCraftAI. Labels include REG4, QPCT, VTN, MITF, COL6A1, VIM, TFAP2A, TYRP, TFAP2B, Rac2, IL1B, Pld1, Aldh5a1, Prt, KRT16, LENG8, Cybb, GNAS, HMGN2, and DKK1. TissueCraftAI is higher for most targets, while Pix2Pix-Turbo appears higher for DKK1.\n\nPanel c: Qualitative visual comparison using an H&E input image and corresponding spatial spot maps. Columns are labeled Ground Truth (KRT16), Pix2Pix-Turbo, and TissueCraftAI. Rows are labeled Raw data, Spot detection, and Kernel Density. The H&E input shows stained tissue/cellular morphology. The raw data and spot detection panels show sparse or clustered spatial dots on a dark background, with detected spots highlighted in red. The kernel density row shows heatmap-like red/yellow density maps. TissueCraftAI visually produces a denser clustered pattern more similar to the ground truth than Pix2Pix-Turbo. Text overlays report KDE similarity and F1 values: Pix2Pix-Turbo KDE similarity -0.05, F1 0; TissueCraftAI KDE similarity 0.50, F1 0.14.\n\nBiological source objects: H&E-stained tissue image and spatial molecular spot distributions, specifically illustrated for KRT16 in panel c.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: H&E input is used to generate or predict spatial spot/raw data distributions, spot detections, and kernel density maps. Models compared are Pix2Pix-Turbo and TissueCraftAI.\n\nMain visible finding: TissueCraftAI outperforms Pix2Pix-Turbo in spot detection F1 score overall and across most listed markers, with a qualitative KRT16 example showing better spatial density agreement with ground truth."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 23, "caption": "Figure 6 -Enhanced prognostic stratification by integrating imputed proteomics with H&E image. (a) Concordance index (Harrell's C -index) of prognostic models trained with H&E-only features versus models that combine H&E features with TissueCraftAI-inferred molecular maps across twenty-three TCGA cancer types: BRCA (Breast Invasive Carcinoma, n=846), CESC (Cervical Squamous Cell Carcinoma and Endocervical Adenocarcinoma, n=307),", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a multi-panel biomedical/scientific figure labeled “Fig. 6” with panels **a**, **b**, and **c**.\n\nPanel **a** shows a grouped bar chart of **Concordance Index** across multiple cancer cohorts: BRCA, CESC, ESCA, GBM, HNSC, KICH, KIRC, KIRP, LGG, LIHC, LUAD, LUSC, OV, PAAD, PCPG, PRAD, READ, SARC, SKCM, STAD, THCA, THYM, and UCEC. Two model/input conditions are compared: **H&E only** in red and **TissueCraftAI combined** in blue. The blue bars are generally higher than the red bars across cohorts.\n\nPanel **b** shows Kaplan-Meier-style survival curves for selected cohorts: **KICH, SARC, SKCM, BRCA, GBM, OV, and LUAD**. Rows compare **H&E only** versus **TissueCraftAI combined**. The y-axis is survival probability and the x-axis is **Event free survival (days)**. Curves appear stratified into high-risk and low-risk groups, with p-values and C-index values shown inside each plot.\n\nPanel **c** shows ROC curves for the same cohorts: **KICH, SARC, SKCM, BRCA, GBM, OV, and LUAD**. The y-axis is **True Positive Rate** and the x-axis is **False Positive Rate**. Red curves represent **H&E only**, and blue curves represent **TissueCraftAI combined**, with AUC values reported in each subplot.\n\nVisible biological source objects/data context: cancer cohort histopathology/survival prediction datasets, likely based on **H&E-stained pathology image inputs** and combined multimodal/model-derived features. No raw microscopy tissue image tiles are shown in this extracted figure; the figure consists of quantitative performance plots.\n\nVisible finding: the **TissueCraftAI combined** condition generally improves concordance index, survival stratification, and ROC/AUC performance compared with **H&E only** across the displayed cancer cohorts."]}]

